    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.maximum_cut - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.maximum_cut.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.maximum_cut()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Split the nodes into two sides so that many edges cross between them\n    (max-cut).\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.maximum_cut()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Split the nodes into two sides so that many edges cross between them\n    (max-cut).\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.maximum_cut()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Split the nodes into two sides so that many edges cross between them\n  (max-cut).\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nSplit the nodes into two sides so that many edges cross between them (max-cut).\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L79327-L79473){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.maximum_cut(\n    method=\"one_exchange\",\n    initial=None,\n    seed=None,\n    weighted=None,\n    p=0.5,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Maximum cut](../assets/graphs/maximum-cut.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A six-node cycle A to F. The split puts alternate nodes on opposite sides: A, C, E on side S (teal) and B, D, F on side T (violet). Every one of the six edges then runs between the two sides, so all six are cut edges in the accent color. The card gives the cut value 6.0 and the two sides.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nThe maximum cut problem asks for a split of the nodes into two groups that maximizes\nthe number (or total weight) of edges with one end in each group. It models choosing\nopposing teams or sides so that conflicts (edges) fall between them, assigning\ncomponents to two circuit layers to minimize same-layer connections, and it is a\nstandard benchmark for optimization. Max-cut is NP-hard, so these methods are\nheuristics:\n\n- `\"one_exchange\"` (default): local search that moves one node to the other side\n  whenever that increases the cut, until no single move helps. The result cuts at least\n  half of the total edge weight (with non-negative weights).\n- `\"randomized\"`: put each node on side `S` with probability `p`. With `p=0.5`, half\n  the weight is cut on average.\n\nEdge direction is ignored, and self-loops never count.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[method]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;one_exchange&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   `\"one_exchange\"` (default) or `\"randomized\"`.\n\n<code>[initial]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list[Union[int, str]] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   One-exchange only: nodes (IDs or labels) that start on side `S`. If `None`\n    (default), all nodes start on side `T`.\n\n<code>[seed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Random seed. One-exchange uses it to break ties between equally good moves.\n\n<code>[weighted]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Whether to use edge weights. If `None` (default), follows the graph's `weighted`\n    flag.\n\n<code>[p]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[0.5]{.fl}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Randomized only: the probability that a node is put on side `S`.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [tuple[float, tuple[list[int], list[int]]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The cut weight and the two sides `(S, T)` as lists of node IDs.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `method=` is unknown, `p` is outside $[0, 1]$, or a node in `initial` does not\n    exist.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nOne-exchange keeps, for every node, the gain of moving it (the weight of its edges to\nits own side minus the weight to the other side) and repeatedly moves a node with the\nlargest positive gain, updating its neighbors' gains in $O(\\deg)$. At a local optimum\nevery node has at least half of its incident weight crossing the cut, so the cut holds\nat least half the total weight. Each move strictly increases the cut, so the search\nterminates. This mirrors NetworkX's `one_exchange` and `randomized_partitioning`.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~minimum_cut`) \n\n:   Separate two nodes with as few crossing edges as possible.\n\n[](`~stoer_wagner`) \n\n:   Find a global minimum cut.\n\n[](`~bipartite_sets`) \n\n:   The two sides of a graph whose every edge can be cut.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nPutting every other node of a 6-node cycle on the other side cuts all 6 edges, but a\ntriangle always has two nodes on the same side, so at most 2 of its 3 edges are cut:\n\n::: {#71c156cf .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\nring = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"D\", \"E\"), (\"E\", \"F\"),\n     (\"F\", \"A\")]\n)\ntriangle = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\")])\n\nring.maximum_cut(seed=1)[0], triangle.maximum_cut(seed=1)[0]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(6.0, 2.0)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Two exam days\n\n`exams()` joins two of the 14 courses at a made-up college when some student takes both,\nso their exams can't share a time slot. If the exams are spread over two days, each\nconflict between two exams on the same day gives some students two exams in one day. The\ncut puts 23 of the 29 conflicts on different days, and no split does better:\n\n::: {#5c7f5cbc .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nexams = ec.exams()\ncut, (first, second) = exams.maximum_cut(seed=1)\nday = {\n    n: \"Monday\" if n in first else \"Tuesday\" for n in exams.get_node_ids()\n}\n\ncut, exams.count_edges()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(23.0, 29)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#da42bb88 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nexams.set_node_attrs(\"day\", day).plot(\n    color_by=\"day\", edge_color_by=\"day\", label_by=\"label\", seed=5\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme